fact WorkItem(id: "WI-211", description: some(value: "Polymorphic type-arg propagation in unify_types (gap B from the WI-209 split). Today unify_types(Stream[T = Term, E = Error], Stream) does not bind Stream's declared type parameters (T, E) to the arg-side bindings. Result: when Stream.head(s: Stream) -> Option[T = T] effects E is called with retrieve(...) returning Stream[T = Term, E = Error], the resulting Option[T = T] still has T unbound (subst doesn't have T -> Term), so the call site sees Option[T = T] and the typer reports type mismatch against expected Option[T = Term]. Fix: when unify_types sees parameterized(Base, [T = X, E = Y]) on one side and a bare sort_ref(Base) on the other, look up Base's declared sort-level type parameters (sort T = ?, sort E = ?) and bind them to X and Y in subst. Effectively treats sort_ref(Base) as parameterized(Base, [<sort T>=fresh-var, <sort E>=fresh-var]) and unifies. Open: how to harvest sort-level type-param names from a sort declaration; whether sort_ref-of-Base should be rewritten at load-time to parameterized form with fresh vars; interaction with typeclass-style dispatch. Driver: WI-203 phase-2 bodies in store.anthill (lookup, by_status_of using Stream.head/Stream.collect on retrieve results) cannot type-check until this lands. Together with WI-209 (parametric effect substitution, landed) it makes those bodies type-check. Acceptance: cargo-test green; the FileBasedWorkitemStore bodies in anthill-todo/store.anthill (next_id, commit, lookup, by_status_of) type-check cleanly under --anthill bundle path; new typing test asserts Stream.head(retrieve(...)) yields Option[T = Term], not Option[T = T]."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-209"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-05-09T21:26:00Z"))
